Body:
We standardize the blockchain core domain model and its serialization to ensure interoperability, stability, and verifiability across nodes and versions.

- Serialization
  - Use Protobuf with deterministic encoding for all on-the-wire and persisted representations.
  - Canonical SignBytes are Protobuf-encoded Canonical* messages with field order fixed by tags and no unknown fields.
  - Backward compatibility via Protobuf field numbering; only additive changes allowed.

- Cryptography and hashing
  - All content hashes use Merkle trees with SHA-256 truncated to 20 bytes (per ADR-010).
  - Addresses are 20 bytes derived from SHA-256(pubkey)[:20].
  - Header/Data/Validators/Evidence root hashes commit to sub-structures via length-prefixed Merkleization.

- Core types
  - Block: {Header, Data{[]Tx}, EvidenceList, LastCommit}
  - Header: {Version{consensus, software}, ChainID, Height(int64), Time(UTC RFC3339Nano), LastBlockID, LastCommitHash, DataHash, ValidatorsHash, NextValidatorsHash, ConsensusHash, AppHash, LastResultsHash, ProposerAddress(20B)}
  - BlockID: {Hash(20B), PartSetHeader}
  - PartSetHeader: {Total(uint32), Hash(20B)}
  - Commit: {Height, Round(int32), BlockID, []CommitSig}
  - CommitSig: {BlockIDFlag, ValidatorAddress(20B), Timestamp, Signature}
  - Vote: {Type(Prevote/Precommit), Height, Round, BlockID, Timestamp, ValidatorAddress, ValidatorIndex, Signature}
  - Tx: opaque bytes; application-defined, length-limited.
  - Evidence: union of DuplicateVoteEvidence and LightClientAttackEvidence; each commits to offending votes/validators and timestamps.
  - Validator: {Address(20B), PubKey, VotingPower(int64), ProposerPriority(int64)}
  - ValidatorSet: {[]Validator, Proposer, TotalVotingPower(int64)}

- Constraints and limits
  - MaxBlockBytes, MaxTxBytes, MaxEvidenceBytes are consensus parameters committed in Header. Enforced during validation.
  - Part size fixed (e.g., 64 KiB) for block propagation; Total computed from Block size.
  - Time must be monotonic and within configured drift bounds.

- Invariants
  - All structs are immutable once signed/hashed.
  - Hash and signature verification are defined over canonical bytes only.
  - Any unknown Protobuf fields are rejected in consensus-critical paths.

We will assign each application installation a universally unique identifier (UUID v4 per RFC 4122) as its Installation ID. The ID will be generated once on first launch, using a cryptographically secure RNG, and persisted locally in the installation’s application data (shared scope for all users of that install). The identifier is immutable for the lifetime of the installation and will be recreated only upon full uninstall/reinstall or explicit reset by an administrator. It will be stored and transmitted in canonical lowercase 8-4-4-4-12 string form.

The Installation ID:
- is non-PII and not derived from device hardware, user identity, MAC addresses, or other stable attributes;
- is not used for authentication, authorization, or access control;
- may be used for telemetry correlation, licensing entitlement checks, rollout/feature flag bucketing, rate limiting, and diagnostics;
- may be included in client logs and outbound requests (e.g., as an HTTP header) to associate events with a single install.

We will provide a supported reset mechanism that rotates the ID and clearly denotes a new installation lineage in server-side systems. Collisions are deemed practically impossible and require no additional coordination. If persistence fails, a new ID will be generated and the event noted to avoid cross-install conflation.
